============================== Start [case_backend,(),{}]
    tensorrt 11.0.0.114
    NetworkDefinitionCreationFlag members: ['PREFER_AOT_PYTHON_PLUGINS', 'PREFER_JIT_PYTHON_PLUGINS', 'STRONGLY_TYPED']
    NetworkDefinitionCreationFlag.EXPLICIT_BATCH exists: False

    `modelopt/onnx/quantization/autotune/benchmark.py:361` does
        self.network_flags = 1 << int(trt.NetworkDefinitionCreationFlag.EXPLICIT_BATCH)
    which raises AttributeError on TensorRT 10+, where explicit batch is the only mode
    and the flag was removed. The autotuner catches it and reports only
        ERROR - Failed to initialize TensorRT benchmark
    without naming the cause. **Every run in this example therefore passes
    `--use_trtexec`**, which takes a different code path and works. This is the same
    family of breakage as the `plugin_creator_list` shim in 05-Plugin/ONNXPTQWithPlugin.
============================== End   [case_backend]
============================== Start [case_autotune,(),{}]
    model-a.onnx: 13 nodes, no Q/DQ yet
    autotuned in 146.2 s, 14 TensorRT benchmarks
    baseline 0.110 ms -> tuned 0.080 ms  (**1.318x**)
    baseline.onnx        :  13 nodes, 0 Q / 0 DQ
    optimized_final.onnx :  35 nodes, 11 Q / 11 DQ

    Note what is being optimized. Both graphs quantize the same tensors; what moves is
    **where the Q/DQ pairs sit relative to the fusions**, and the only way to know which
    placement wins is to build the engine and time it. That is the whole idea: the
    objective function is a real TensorRT latency, not a proxy like 'quantize more ops'.
    Contrast 07-Tool/FP16Tuning, which searches precision per layer against *accuracy*.
============================== End   [case_autotune]
============================== Start [case_regions_and_patterns,(),{}]
    baseline_latency_ms: 0.109863
    performance_threshold: 1.02   <- a scheme must beat the best by this factor to be adopted

    3 pattern(s) discovered, each a sub-graph the search treats as one unit:
      size 3: Conv -> Relu -> Conv                          4 scheme(s), best 0.0975 ms
      size 5: Conv -> Relu -> Conv -> Relu -> MaxPool       4 scheme(s), best 0.0627 ms
      size 13: COMPOSITE(Conv -> Relu -> Conv -> Relu -> Conv -> Relu -> MaxPool -> Relu -> Conv -> Relu -> MaxPool 4 scheme(s), best 0.0831 ms

    A pattern signature is the op sequence *with its attributes*:
      Conv[dilations=1x1,group=1,kernel_shape=3x3,pads=1x1x1x1,strides=1x1]->Relu->Conv[dilations=1x1,group=1,kernel...
    so `Conv[kernel_shape=3x3]` and `Conv[kernel_shape=1x1]` are different patterns and
    never share a result. That is what makes the cache below safe to reuse across models.
============================== End   [case_regions_and_patterns]
============================== Start [case_pattern_cache,(),{}]
    cache from model-a: 3 pattern(s), 4 scheme(s), minimum_distance=4

    tuning model-b        benchmarks   wall time   speedup found
    --------------------------------------------------------------
    without the cache             14     142.4 s   1.297x
    with model-a's cache          14     134.7 s   1.298x

    The cache is keyed by pattern signature, so the shared conv blocks are recognised
    even though model-b has a different head. What it saves is benchmarks -- and a
    benchmark is a full engine build plus a timing run, which is the expensive part.
    **Here it saved nothing**: 14 benchmarks either way. Both models are
    small enough that the search explores every scheme regardless. The mechanism is
    real; the benefit needs a model with more repeated structure than this one.
============================== End   [case_pattern_cache]

